1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,252
Not every database job happens in a terminal.

2
00:00:03,802 --> 00:00:06,911
A teammate asks: how many orders came in last week?

3
00:00:06,911 --> 00:00:09,532
A manager wants to see the customers table.

4
00:00:09,532 --> 00:00:12,335
A new engineer wants to understand the schema.

5
00:00:12,885 --> 00:00:16,981
You could write queries for each of them. Or you can open a web page, and look.

6
00:00:17,531 --> 00:00:20,483
Today you tour a real database user interface,

7
00:00:20,483 --> 00:00:21,510
called admin er,

8
00:00:21,510 --> 00:00:23,307
on a real post gress server,

9
00:00:23,307 --> 00:00:25,553
and learn every part of the screen.

10
00:00:26,103 --> 00:00:31,180
A user interface is fast and friendly. It is also powerful, so you learn it with care.

11
00:00:32,730 --> 00:00:34,159
Here is what to expect.

12
00:00:34,709 --> 00:00:39,104
One: why teams use a database user interface, and which tools exist.

13
00:00:39,654 --> 00:00:41,069
Two: an anatomy lesson.

14
00:00:41,069 --> 00:00:42,545
A picture of the screen,

15
00:00:42,545 --> 00:00:43,898
with every zone named,

16
00:00:43,898 --> 00:00:45,435
before we touch anything.

17
00:00:45,985 --> 00:00:47,461
Three: a live tour,

18
00:00:47,461 --> 00:00:48,626
click by click.

19
00:00:48,626 --> 00:00:49,092
Login,

20
00:00:49,092 --> 00:00:49,636
tables,

21
00:00:49,636 --> 00:00:50,413
structure,

22
00:00:50,413 --> 00:00:50,801
data,

23
00:00:50,801 --> 00:00:51,189
edit,

24
00:00:51,189 --> 00:00:51,655
S Q L,

25
00:00:51,655 --> 00:00:52,199
export,

26
00:00:52,199 --> 00:00:53,985
and the schema diagram.

27
00:00:54,535 --> 00:00:57,970
Four: five habits for using a database interface safely.

28
00:00:58,520 --> 00:00:59,471
Then your turn,

29
00:00:59,471 --> 00:01:02,259
with a lab that judges you with five checks.

30
00:01:02,259 --> 00:01:04,984
This is lesson six of the databases domain.

31
00:01:06,834 --> 00:01:08,925
Why use a database user interface?

32
00:01:09,475 --> 00:01:10,016
To look.

33
00:01:10,016 --> 00:01:10,963
Browse tables,

34
00:01:10,963 --> 00:01:11,911
see real rows,

35
00:01:11,911 --> 00:01:14,550
understand a schema you did not design.

36
00:01:14,550 --> 00:01:17,731
Much faster than typing select a hundred times.

37
00:01:18,281 --> 00:01:23,763
To make small, careful changes. Fix one typo in one row. Add one test customer.

38
00:01:24,313 --> 00:01:25,757
To explore and export.

39
00:01:25,757 --> 00:01:26,938
Run a quick query,

40
00:01:26,938 --> 00:01:30,415
and download the answer as a C S V for a spreadsheet.

41
00:01:30,965 --> 00:01:32,909
Three tools you will meet.

42
00:01:32,909 --> 00:01:34,778
Admin er: one P H P file,

43
00:01:34,778 --> 00:01:35,152
tiny,

44
00:01:35,152 --> 00:01:36,947
supports many databases.

45
00:01:36,947 --> 00:01:40,611
P G admin: the full official tool for post gress.

46
00:01:40,611 --> 00:01:45,621
And dee beaver: a desktop program that connects to almost anything.

47
00:01:46,171 --> 00:01:47,183
We use admin er,

48
00:01:47,183 --> 00:01:50,854
because it installs in seconds and every screen is simple.

49
00:01:50,854 --> 00:01:53,701
Every idea you learn transfers to the others.

50
00:01:54,251 --> 00:01:57,466
A user interface does not replace the command line.

51
00:01:57,466 --> 00:01:57,970
Scripts,

52
00:01:57,970 --> 00:02:00,743
backups and automation stay in the terminal.

53
00:02:00,743 --> 00:02:02,507
The interface is for people,

54
00:02:02,507 --> 00:02:03,894
looking and exploring.

55
00:02:05,444 --> 00:02:10,671
Anatomy of the screen. Learn these six zones, and you can use any database interface.

56
00:02:11,221 --> 00:02:11,804
Zone one,

57
00:02:11,804 --> 00:02:12,841
the bread crumb,

58
00:02:12,841 --> 00:02:13,553
at the top.

59
00:02:13,553 --> 00:02:15,821
It shows where you are: the server,

60
00:02:15,821 --> 00:02:16,664
the database,

61
00:02:16,664 --> 00:02:17,376
the schema,

62
00:02:17,376 --> 00:02:18,024
the table.

63
00:02:18,024 --> 00:02:19,903
Every part is a link back up.

64
00:02:20,453 --> 00:02:23,803
Zone two, the user name and the logout button, top right.

65
00:02:24,353 --> 00:02:25,035
Zone three,

66
00:02:25,035 --> 00:02:27,145
the database and schema selectors,

67
00:02:27,145 --> 00:02:28,325
top of the sidebar.

68
00:02:28,325 --> 00:02:31,179
They decide which database you are looking at.

69
00:02:31,729 --> 00:02:32,454
Zone four,

70
00:02:32,454 --> 00:02:34,843
the main menu and the table list,

71
00:02:34,843 --> 00:02:36,292
also in the sidebar.

72
00:02:36,292 --> 00:02:37,306
S Q L command,

73
00:02:37,306 --> 00:02:37,813
import,

74
00:02:37,813 --> 00:02:38,320
export,

75
00:02:38,320 --> 00:02:39,985
and one link per table.

76
00:02:40,535 --> 00:02:41,255
Zone five,

77
00:02:41,255 --> 00:02:41,903
the tabs,

78
00:02:41,903 --> 00:02:43,415
under the page title.

79
00:02:43,415 --> 00:02:44,279
Select data,

80
00:02:44,279 --> 00:02:45,359
show structure,

81
00:02:45,359 --> 00:02:46,223
alter table,

82
00:02:46,223 --> 00:02:46,871
new item.

83
00:02:47,421 --> 00:02:47,997
Zone six,

84
00:02:47,997 --> 00:02:48,957
the main panel.

85
00:02:48,957 --> 00:02:50,493
This is where the table,

86
00:02:50,493 --> 00:02:51,069
the rows,

87
00:02:51,069 --> 00:02:52,349
or the form appears.

88
00:02:52,349 --> 00:02:54,333
Everything you do ends up here.

89
00:02:54,883 --> 00:02:58,041
Remember this picture. We now visit every zone, live.

90
00:02:59,591 --> 00:03:03,026
Start in the terminal. Start the lab. Type: lab, up.

91
00:03:03,576 --> 00:03:06,121
This starts a private post gress server,

92
00:03:06,121 --> 00:03:07,394
loads the shop data,

93
00:03:07,394 --> 00:03:09,558
and serves admin er on a web port.

94
00:03:09,558 --> 00:03:11,085
Wait for the ready line.

95
00:03:16,635 --> 00:03:19,259
Now the shortcut U I. Type: U I.

96
00:03:19,809 --> 00:03:20,575
Where to go,

97
00:03:20,575 --> 00:03:22,554
and how to log in: the address,

98
00:03:22,554 --> 00:03:23,256
the system,

99
00:03:23,256 --> 00:03:24,533
the server and port,

100
00:03:24,533 --> 00:03:25,108
the user,

101
00:03:25,108 --> 00:03:25,937
the password,

102
00:03:25,937 --> 00:03:27,023
and the database.

103
00:03:27,023 --> 00:03:28,236
Keep these in mind.

104
00:03:28,786 --> 00:03:32,498
Now the browser. Open local host, port eighty-eighty-five.

105
00:03:37,948 --> 00:03:39,038
The login page.

106
00:03:39,038 --> 00:03:41,800
Admin er works with several databases,

107
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:44,199
so the first field is the system.

108
00:03:44,199 --> 00:03:45,798
Choose post gress Q L.

109
00:03:46,348 --> 00:03:49,488
Server: the address and port of the database.

110
00:03:49,488 --> 00:03:52,488
One twenty-seven dot zero dot zero dot one,

111
00:03:52,488 --> 00:03:54,092
port fifty-five eleven.

112
00:03:58,542 --> 00:03:59,429
The user name,

113
00:03:59,429 --> 00:04:00,126
shop admin.

114
00:04:00,126 --> 00:04:00,949
The password,

115
00:04:00,949 --> 00:04:02,912
which stays hidden as you type.

116
00:04:02,912 --> 00:04:04,306
And the database name,

117
00:04:04,306 --> 00:04:04,622
shop.

118
00:04:22,272 --> 00:04:23,296
Click login.

119
00:04:25,146 --> 00:04:27,421
The home screen of the shop schema.

120
00:04:27,421 --> 00:04:30,605
First the bread crumb at the top: post gress Q L,

121
00:04:30,605 --> 00:04:31,320
the server,

122
00:04:31,320 --> 00:04:32,490
the database shop,

123
00:04:32,490 --> 00:04:33,660
the schema public.

124
00:04:33,660 --> 00:04:34,960
Each part is a link.

125
00:04:35,510 --> 00:04:37,711
At the top right: the logged in user,

126
00:04:37,711 --> 00:04:39,021
and the logout button.

127
00:04:39,021 --> 00:04:40,270
On a shared computer,

128
00:04:40,270 --> 00:04:41,163
always log out.

129
00:04:41,713 --> 00:04:42,614
In the sidebar,

130
00:04:42,614 --> 00:04:45,377
the selectors for the database and the schema.

131
00:04:45,377 --> 00:04:46,998
Before you change anything,

132
00:04:46,998 --> 00:04:47,959
check these two.

133
00:04:47,959 --> 00:04:48,860
Wrong database,

134
00:04:48,860 --> 00:04:49,521
wrong data.

135
00:04:50,071 --> 00:04:52,565
Then the main menu: S Q L command,

136
00:04:52,565 --> 00:04:53,079
import,

137
00:04:53,079 --> 00:04:53,592
export,

138
00:04:53,592 --> 00:04:54,546
create table.

139
00:04:54,546 --> 00:04:55,206
Below it,

140
00:04:55,206 --> 00:04:57,187
one line per table or view.

141
00:04:57,187 --> 00:04:59,167
Order totals is in italics,

142
00:04:59,167 --> 00:05:00,708
because it is a view,

143
00:05:00,708 --> 00:05:01,735
a saved query,

144
00:05:01,735 --> 00:05:02,615
not a table.

145
00:05:03,165 --> 00:05:06,211
The main panel lists the tables and the views.

146
00:05:06,211 --> 00:05:07,932
Engine says table or view.

147
00:05:07,932 --> 00:05:11,309
Data length and index length are the bytes on disk.

148
00:05:11,309 --> 00:05:14,090
Rows is an estimate: about four customers,

149
00:05:14,090 --> 00:05:15,017
twelve orders,

150
00:05:15,017 --> 00:05:15,944
five products.

151
00:05:16,494 --> 00:05:20,009
Search data in tables looks for a word in every table at once.

152
00:05:20,009 --> 00:05:22,787
Handy when you do not know where something lives.

153
00:05:23,337 --> 00:05:24,384
Below the list,

154
00:05:24,384 --> 00:05:25,849
four buttons: vacuum,

155
00:05:25,849 --> 00:05:26,477
optimize,

156
00:05:26,477 --> 00:05:27,733
truncate and drop.

157
00:05:27,733 --> 00:05:30,385
They stay grey until you tick a table.

158
00:05:30,385 --> 00:05:32,129
Truncate empties a table.

159
00:05:32,129 --> 00:05:33,246
Drop deletes it.

160
00:05:33,246 --> 00:05:34,153
Respect them.

161
00:05:34,703 --> 00:05:37,391
Now open the orders table. Click its name.

162
00:05:38,241 --> 00:05:39,297
The table page.

163
00:05:39,297 --> 00:05:40,917
Four tabs: select data,

164
00:05:40,917 --> 00:05:41,973
show structure,

165
00:05:41,973 --> 00:05:42,818
alter table,

166
00:05:42,818 --> 00:05:43,734
and new item.

167
00:05:43,734 --> 00:05:45,494
We are on show structure.

168
00:05:46,044 --> 00:05:46,867
The columns,

169
00:05:46,867 --> 00:05:47,964
and their types.

170
00:05:47,964 --> 00:05:53,380
Id is an integer with auto increment: the database fills it in from a sequence.

171
00:05:53,380 --> 00:05:54,614
Qty has a default,

172
00:05:54,614 --> 00:05:55,986
the one in brackets.

173
00:05:56,536 --> 00:05:57,103
Indexes.

174
00:05:57,103 --> 00:05:58,664
Primary on id: unique,

175
00:05:58,664 --> 00:05:59,870
and fast to find.

176
00:05:59,870 --> 00:06:02,423
Alter indexes is where you add more.

177
00:06:02,423 --> 00:06:05,048
Remember it: it is part of your task.

178
00:06:05,598 --> 00:06:06,539
Foreign keys.

179
00:06:06,539 --> 00:06:08,929
Customer I D points to customers.

180
00:06:08,929 --> 00:06:11,173
Product I D points to products.

181
00:06:11,173 --> 00:06:15,880
The database refuses an order for a customer that does not exist.

182
00:06:16,430 --> 00:06:18,777
Now click select data, to see the rows.

183
00:06:20,227 --> 00:06:20,728
On top,

184
00:06:20,728 --> 00:06:25,521
admin er shows the S Q L it wrote for you: select star from orders,

185
00:06:25,521 --> 00:06:26,380
limit fifty,

186
00:06:26,380 --> 00:06:27,882
and how long it took.

187
00:06:27,882 --> 00:06:30,744
You can learn S Q L just by watching it.

188
00:06:31,294 --> 00:06:32,552
The row of boxes.

189
00:06:32,552 --> 00:06:34,550
Select chooses the columns.

190
00:06:34,550 --> 00:06:36,326
Search filters the rows.

191
00:06:36,326 --> 00:06:37,584
Sort orders them.

192
00:06:37,584 --> 00:06:39,285
Limit is how many rows.

193
00:06:39,285 --> 00:06:41,579
Text length shortens long text.

194
00:06:41,579 --> 00:06:42,985
And Action runs it.

195
00:06:43,535 --> 00:06:46,179
Each blue number in customer I D is a link.

196
00:06:46,179 --> 00:06:48,085
Click the one in the first row,

197
00:06:48,085 --> 00:06:51,343
and admin er follows the foreign key to the customer.

198
00:06:51,893 --> 00:06:54,309
Order one belongs to customer one,

199
00:06:54,309 --> 00:06:54,593
Ana,

200
00:06:54,593 --> 00:06:55,233
in Lagos.

201
00:06:55,233 --> 00:06:57,862
This page filtered by a where clause,

202
00:06:57,862 --> 00:06:59,212
shown in the S Q L.

203
00:06:59,212 --> 00:07:01,344
Click edit to change this row.

204
00:07:05,494 --> 00:07:06,496
The edit form.

205
00:07:06,496 --> 00:07:08,215
One box for each column.

206
00:07:08,215 --> 00:07:09,719
We move Ana to Abuja.

207
00:07:09,719 --> 00:07:11,080
Click the city box,

208
00:07:11,080 --> 00:07:11,868
select all,

209
00:07:11,868 --> 00:07:13,515
and type the new value.

210
00:07:14,065 --> 00:07:19,569
Scroll down. Three buttons. Save. Save and continue edit. And Delete. Click save.

211
00:07:22,519 --> 00:07:25,051
A green message: item has been updated.

212
00:07:25,051 --> 00:07:26,609
The row now shows Abuja.

213
00:07:26,609 --> 00:07:28,427
That is an update statement,

214
00:07:28,427 --> 00:07:29,986
written and run for you.

215
00:07:30,536 --> 00:07:32,495
Now the S Q L command page.

216
00:07:32,495 --> 00:07:34,236
Click it in the sidebar.

217
00:07:34,236 --> 00:07:38,953
It is a plain editor: anything you can type in P S Q L runs here.

218
00:07:38,953 --> 00:07:41,202
We query the order totals view.

219
00:07:43,952 --> 00:07:48,752
Press control enter, or the execute button. The answer appears above the editor.

220
00:07:54,202 --> 00:07:56,134
Under the table: how many rows,

221
00:07:56,134 --> 00:07:57,193
how long it took,

222
00:07:57,193 --> 00:07:58,938
and links to edit the query,

223
00:07:58,938 --> 00:07:59,624
explain it,

224
00:07:59,624 --> 00:08:00,434
or export it.

225
00:08:00,434 --> 00:08:01,493
Below the editor,

226
00:08:01,493 --> 00:08:03,425
history remembers your queries,

227
00:08:03,425 --> 00:08:06,042
and stop on error protects longer scripts.

228
00:08:06,592 --> 00:08:07,367
Now export.

229
00:08:07,367 --> 00:08:09,337
Click export in the sidebar.

230
00:08:09,337 --> 00:08:11,941
Output: open shows it in the browser,

231
00:08:11,941 --> 00:08:13,490
save downloads a file,

232
00:08:13,490 --> 00:08:14,827
gzip compresses it.

233
00:08:29,477 --> 00:08:30,495
Format: S Q L,

234
00:08:30,495 --> 00:08:32,530
for a backup you can replay,

235
00:08:32,530 --> 00:08:33,910
or C S V and T S V,

236
00:08:33,910 --> 00:08:35,219
for a spreadsheet.

237
00:08:35,219 --> 00:08:39,434
Tables: drop plus create makes the file rebuild the table.

238
00:08:39,434 --> 00:08:41,542
Data: insert writes the rows.

239
00:08:41,542 --> 00:08:44,304
You can untick tables you do not need.

240
00:08:44,854 --> 00:08:45,824
Click export.

241
00:08:45,824 --> 00:08:49,330
An S Q L dump appears: create table statements,

242
00:08:49,330 --> 00:08:51,046
then insert statements.

243
00:08:51,046 --> 00:08:54,552
The same idea as P G dump from the last lesson,

244
00:08:54,552 --> 00:08:55,671
in the browser.

245
00:08:55,671 --> 00:08:56,940
For real backups,

246
00:08:56,940 --> 00:08:57,910
use P G dump.

247
00:08:58,460 --> 00:09:00,127
One more screen: indexes,

248
00:09:00,127 --> 00:09:01,928
because your task needs it.

249
00:09:01,928 --> 00:09:03,261
Click select orders,

250
00:09:03,261 --> 00:09:04,595
then show structure,

251
00:09:04,595 --> 00:09:05,862
then alter indexes.

252
00:09:09,512 --> 00:09:10,396
A small form.

253
00:09:10,396 --> 00:09:11,144
Index type,

254
00:09:11,144 --> 00:09:11,960
the columns,

255
00:09:11,960 --> 00:09:12,844
and the name.

256
00:09:12,844 --> 00:09:15,088
The first row is the primary key.

257
00:09:15,088 --> 00:09:17,807
A new index goes in the empty row below.

258
00:09:17,807 --> 00:09:20,595
We will not save here: that is your task.

259
00:09:20,595 --> 00:09:21,139
Go back.

260
00:09:21,689 --> 00:09:25,550
And new item. Open select orders again, and click new item.

261
00:09:27,600 --> 00:09:29,716
A blank form to insert one row.

262
00:09:29,716 --> 00:09:31,148
Id is auto increment,

263
00:09:31,148 --> 00:09:32,377
so leave it empty.

264
00:09:32,377 --> 00:09:34,355
The other boxes are required.

265
00:09:34,355 --> 00:09:36,198
We close it without saving.

266
00:09:36,748 --> 00:09:38,104
Now the schema picture.

267
00:09:38,104 --> 00:09:40,404
Go up to the schema in the bread crumb,

268
00:09:40,404 --> 00:09:41,996
then click database schema.

269
00:09:47,746 --> 00:09:50,102
Admin er draws the tables as boxes,

270
00:09:50,102 --> 00:09:52,323
with arrows for the foreign keys.

271
00:09:52,323 --> 00:09:55,218
Orders points to customers and to products.

272
00:09:55,218 --> 00:09:57,708
A free diagram of the whole database.

273
00:09:58,258 --> 00:09:58,786
Finally,

274
00:09:58,786 --> 00:09:59,313
log out.

275
00:09:59,313 --> 00:10:00,170
Click logout.

276
00:10:00,170 --> 00:10:01,620
The session is closed.

277
00:10:01,620 --> 00:10:03,268
The lab is still running,

278
00:10:03,268 --> 00:10:05,576
so you can try everything yourself.

279
00:10:23,726 --> 00:10:26,819
Five habits for using a database interface safely.

280
00:10:27,369 --> 00:10:30,329
One: look at the bread crumb before every change.

281
00:10:30,329 --> 00:10:31,114
Right server,

282
00:10:31,114 --> 00:10:32,020
right database,

283
00:10:32,020 --> 00:10:32,745
right table.

284
00:10:33,295 --> 00:10:38,543
